WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.227
Where do Q, K, and V actually come from?

00:00:03.227 --> 00:00:06.747
They are learned transformations of the input vectors.

00:00:06.747 --> 00:00:11.697
Suppose the matrix X contains the token representations for one sequence.

00:00:11.697 --> 00:00:17.227
The attention layer contains three parameter matrices, commonly written as W Q, W K, and W V.

00:00:17.227 --> 00:00:19.762
Multiplying X by W Q creates the Query matrix.

00:00:19.762 --> 00:00:23.762
Multiplying X by W K creates the Key matrix.

00:00:23.762 --> 00:00:27.452
Multiplying X by W V creates the Value matrix.

00:00:27.452 --> 00:00:33.912
These parameter matrices are initialized when the neural network is created and updated during training

00:00:33.912 --> 00:00:37.652
through backpropagation and gradient-based optimization.

00:00:37.652 --> 00:00:40.517
We do not calculate them manually for each sentence.

00:00:40.517 --> 00:00:46.802
The same token representation is therefore viewed in three different learned spaces:

00:00:46.802 --> 00:00:51.362
one for asking what information is needed, one for matching against other tokens,

00:00:51.362 --> 00:00:56.782
and one for carrying the information that will be mixed.

00:00:56.782 --> 00:01:02.480
When we add a library attention layer, these trainable projections are already built into that layer.
